<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artsakh (historic province)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artsakh is the historical name of a mountainous region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">South Caucasus</a> corresponding largely to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh">Nagorno-Karabakh</a>. A province of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in antiquity, it passed to Caucasian Albania after the partition of Armenia around 387. Under Armenian princes such as those of Khachen it kept a strong Christian culture with monasteries like Gandzasar. The name was revived for the unrecognised republic of 1991–2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artsakh-historic-province.jpg" alt="Artsakh (historic province)" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original author: User Hovik95, I colored Artsakh green. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Artsakh_within_Armenian_Kingdom.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Azerbaijan then blockaded <b>Artsakh</b> until food and medicine ran short and on 19–20 September 2023 launched the offensive that ended it; the European Parliament called it a violation of the 2020 ceasefire, genocide scholars warned of ethnic cleansing, and almost the entire population fled.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In September 2023 an offensive ended the Republic of <b>Artsakh</b>, dissolved on 1 January 2024, and nearly all ethnic Armenians were expelled from the region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After the partition of Armenia between Rome and Persia around 387, Albania received the former Armenian provinces of <b>Artsakh</b> and Utik south of the Kura, which greatly enlarged it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Today Armenian is official in Armenia, was official in the Republic of <b>Artsakh</b>, and is recognised as an official language of the Eurasian Economic Union.</blockquote><a href="/language/armenian">Armenian · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Khachen emerged in the 9th century in the mountains of <b>Artsakh</b>, in the former lands of the Mihranid Principality of Albania.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-khachen">Principality of Khachen</a></li></ul></section>
